{{Politics of Poland}}

Presidential elections were held in [[Poland]] on 20 June 2010. As no candidate received a majority of votes in the first round, a second round was held on 4 July 2010.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/europe/8633981.stm |title=Poland's snap presidential elections set for June 20 |work=BBC News |date=21 April 2010 |access-date=21 June 2010}}</ref> [[Bronisław Komorowski]], the [[acting President of Poland]] and vice-chairman of the [[Civic Platform]], defeated [[Jarosław Kaczyński]], twin brother of the recently deceased President [[Lech Kaczyński]] and chairman of [[Law and Justice (Poland)|Law and Justice]] (PiS). The [[2008 financial crisis]], [[2010 Central European floods|flooding in Poland]] and the [[2010 Polish Air Force Tu-154 crash|Smolensk disaster]] were the main themes in the last months of the election campaign.

{{As of|{{CURRENTYEAR}}}}, this remains the last Polish presidential election won by a non PiS-aligned candidate and the only election won by a candidate from PO.

==Background==
The 2010 presidential election was expected to be a rematch of the [[2005 Polish presidential election|2005 election]] between [[Lech Kaczyński]] and [[Donald Tusk]]. Since the last presidential election, Polish politics had become centered around the rivalry between [[Law and Justice (Poland)|Law and Justice]] and the [[Civic Platform]]. President Kaczyński and Prime Minister Tusk were constantly battling each other over who should represent Poland on the international stage.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://euobserver.com/political/26658|title = Spats over who gets to go to EU summit break out in Poland, Finland| date=29 August 2008 }}</ref> Tusk was leading Kaczyński in all the opinion polls (as he did ahead of the 2005 elections, which he ultimately lost) and the election was expected to be a dirty one. However, this rematch never came to be. Donald Tusk announced in January 2010 that he will not run for President because he wants to remain Prime Minister.<ref name="Polishmarket.com.pl">{{cite web |url=http://www.polishmarket.com.pl/document/:22912?p=%2FEconomic+Monitor%2F |title=Polish Market Online .:. Polish Market Online .:. What's next for Poland? |publisher=Polishmarket.com.pl |date=12 April 2010 |access-date=21 June 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111005213515/http://www.polishmarket.com.pl/document/:22912?p=%2FEconomic+Monitor%2F |archive-date=5 October 2011 |url-status=dead  }}</ref> Lech Kaczyński was set to declare his candidacy for re-election on 23 May but he died on 10 April in the [[Smolensk air disaster]], while he was attending the remembrance for the 70th anniversary of the [[Katyn massacre]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.tokfm.pl/Tokfm/1,103087,7745826,Wybory_2010__Lech_Kaczynski_rozpocznie_kampanie_23.html|title=Wybory 2010. Lech Kaczyński rozpocznie kampanię 23 maja}}</ref>

After Donald Tusk declined to run for president, the Civic Platform organized a [[2010 Civic Platform presidential primary|presidential primary]]. The primary was won by [[Bronisław Komorowski]] who defeated [[Radosław Sikorski]] receiving 68.5% of the vote to Sikorski's 31.5%.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.newsweek.pl/polska/komorowski-kandydatem-po-na-prezydenta-kleska-sikorskiego/2dwvxfn|title=Komorowski kandydatem PO na prezydenta. Klęska Sikorskiego|date=27 March 2010 }}</ref> Bronisław Komorowski consistently lead Lech Kaczyński by double digits in the first round and by a 2-to-1 margin in the second round. No other candidate registered support in the double digits.<ref>https://www.money.pl/gospodarka/polityka/artykul/sondaz;kaczynski;nie;ma;szans;na;reelekcje,22,0,603670.htm {{Dead link|date=February 2022}}</ref>

After President [[Lech Kaczyński]]'s death in a plane crash, the Constitution required the [[Marshal of the Sejm]] to declare the date within two weeks, with the election to take place on a weekend within the following 60 days, i.e. 20 June at the latest. On 21 April, the Marshal, [[Bronisław Komorowski]], announced the election date as 20 June 2010.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.businessweek.com/news/2010-04-14/poland-may-hold-early-election-on-june-20-napieralski-says.html |title=Poland May Hold Election June 20, Napieralski Says (Update1) |work=Bloomberg BusinessWeek |date=14 April 2010 |access-date=21 June 2010}}{{dead link|date=April 2023|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}</ref> Candidates were required to register by 26 April 2010 (with 1,000 signatures of voters in support) and submit 100,000 signatures by 6 May 2010.<ref>{{cite news|last=Sobczyk |first=Marcin |url=https://www.wsj.com/articles/SB10001424052748704133804575197321643319484?mod=WSJ_latestheadlines |title=Polish Election Set for June 20 |work=The Wall Street Journal |date=21 April 2010 |access-date=21 June 2010}}</ref>

Originally, Lech was up for re-election between 19 September and 3 October; the exact date would have been announced between 23 May and 23 June, before the end of his first five-year term of office.<ref>[http://www.sejm.gov.pl/prawo/konst/angielski/kon1.htm#5 Constitution of Poland], Article 128</ref>

Two candidates for the election died in the crash. Incumbent Lech Kaczyński was nominated as the [[Law and Justice (Poland)|Law and Justice]] party candidate (he had yet to accept the nomination, but it was widely believed that he would do so), and [[Jerzy Szmajdziński]] was to have run for the [[Democratic Left Alliance (Poland)|Democratic Left Alliance]].<ref>{{cite web | title = Jerzy Szmajdziński – kandydat SLD na prezydenta | work = Gazeta Wyborcza | date = 20 December 2009 | url = http://wyborcza.pl/1,75478,7385246,Jerzy_Szmajdzinski___kandydat_SLD_na_prezydenta.html | access-date = 22 December 2009 |language=pl}}</ref> [[Jarosław Kaczyński]] ran for president as the replacement for his deceased twin brother.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-poland-election-view/instant-view-jaroslaw-kaczynski-to-run-for-polish-presidency-idUSTRE63P1RR20100426|title=Instant view: Jaroslaw Kaczynski to run for Polish presidency|newspaper=Reuters|date=26 April 2010}}</ref> Jerzy Szmajdziński's replacement was [[Grzegorz Napieralski]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.newsweek.pl/grzegorz-napieralski-kandydat-na-prezydenta-sld-wybory-2010/z22cefj|title = Grzegorz Napieralski| date=20 May 2010 }}</ref>

[[2010 Polish Senate by-elections|Senate by-elections]] to fill the three seats whose senators died in the crash – [[Krystyna Bochenek]] (PO), [[Janina Fetlińska]] (PiS) and [[Stanisław Zając]] (PiS, himself elected in a by-election on 22 June 2008 to replace [[Andrzej Mazurkiewicz (politician)|Andrzej Mazurkiewicz]]) – were held on the same day.<ref name="Polishmarket.com.pl"/>

== Candidates ==
In total, 23 candidates registered with the Polish National Electoral Commission on, or just following, the 26 April deadline. 17 of the 23 candidacies were accepted by the commission, whilst the remaining 6 were rejected because they had not gathered the required 1000 signatures.

Only ten candidates collected the required 100,000 signatures and were eligible to run for president.<ref>{{cite web|author=jagor, zsz |url=http://wiadomosci.gazeta.pl/Wiadomosci/1,80271,7851073,Wybory_2010__Dziewieciu_kandydatom_na_prezydenta_udalo.html |title=Wybory 2010. Dziesięciu kandydatom na prezydenta udało się zebrać 100 tys. podpisów |publisher=Wiadomosci.gazeta.pl |access-date=21 June 2010|language=pl}}</ref>

