== Results ==
[[File:Präsidentschaftswahl Polen 2010 Runde 1.svg|thumb|255px|Results of the first round]]
[[File:Voting card Polish presidential elections 2010 Poznan.jpg|thumb|220px|Second round voting card]]

There were 10 candidates in the first round of voting. [[Bronisław Komorowski]] of [[Civic Platform]] received 41.5% and [[Jarosław Kaczyński]] of [[Law and Justice (Poland)|Law and Justice]] received 36.5%, causing a second round of voting between the two. The other eight candidates were eliminated.<ref name="news.bbc.co.uk">{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/10503555|title=Bronislaw Komorowski declared president of Poland|date=5 July 2010|work=[[BBC News]]|publisher=[[BBC]]|access-date=5 July 2010}}</ref>

Soon after exit polls were released in the second round, [[Jarosław Kaczyński]] conceded that he had been defeated. [[Bronisław Komorowski]] appeared to tentatively claim victory, stating: "Tonight we will open a small bottle of champagne and tomorrow we will open a big bottle."<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/10503555|title=Kaczynski accepts Polish election defeat|date=4 July 2010|work=[[BBC News]]|publisher=[[BBC]]|access-date=4 July 2010}}</ref> The exit polls put Komorowski ahead of Kaczyński by 53% to 47%.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.abc.net.au/news/stories/2010/07/05/2944462.htm?section=world|archive-url=https://archive.today/20120722031150/http://www.abc.net.au/news/stories/2010/07/05/2944462.htm?section=world|url-status=dead|archive-date=22 July 2012|title=Liberal beats bereaved twin in Polish vote|date=5 July 2010|work=[[ABC News (Australia)|ABC News]]|publisher=[[Australian Broadcasting Corporation]]|access-date=4 July 2010}}</ref> On the following day, Komorowski was declared the winner of the election.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.google.com/hostednews/ap/article/ALeqM5ir8JleEyG0Yo_1nhoC5R225rcGjAD9GP26B80|title=Komorowski win strengthens Polish government|last=Gera|first=Vanessa|date=5 July 2010|agency=[[Associated Press]]|access-date=5 July 2010}}{{dead link|date=June 2024|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}</ref> The final result put Komorowski with 53.01% of the vote, and Kaczyński with 46.99%.<ref name="news.bbc.co.uk"/>
Komorowski's win resulted in Civic Platform holding both the Presidency and the government (under Prime Minister [[Donald Tusk]]).<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/worldnews/europe/poland/7873488/Polands-liberals-dominate-after-dead-presidents-twin-defeated.html|title=Poland's liberals dominate after dead president's twin defeated|date=5 July 2010|work=[[The Daily Telegraph]]|access-date=5 July 2010 | location=London | first=Our | last=Foreign}}</ref> Correspondents in US and British business newspapers suggested that Komorowski's win would mean closer engagement with the [[European Union]],<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.wsj.com/articles/SB10001424052748703620604575348772126843284?mod=googlenews_wsj|title=Poland Vote Strengthens European Ties|last=Fairclough|first=Gordon|date=5 July 2010|work=[[The Wall Street Journal]]|access-date=5 July 2010}}</ref> and such domestic economic reforms as deficit reduction.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.ft.com/content/12d2922a-8859-11df-aade-00144feabdc0|title=Poland poised for vital reforms|last=Buckley|first=Neil|date=5 July 2010|work=[[Financial Times]]|publisher=[[Pearson PLC]]|access-date=5 July 2010}}</ref>

